Startup cost -- and why it differs by 100x:

  All three native runtimes reserve the same 16M-slot (128 MB) heap. The cost of
  doing so is not the same, and it is the whole startup gap:

    ic32 (C)     calloc()                      -> kernel lazy zero pages, untouched
    ic32 (Zig)   gpa.alloc + @memset(heap, 0)  -> eagerly writes all 128 MB
    ic32 (Mojo)  List[UInt64](.., fill=0)      -> eagerly writes all 128 MB

  measured: (C) 0.45ms, (Zig) 30ms, (Mojo) 46ms, ic32.wasm 28ms
  [2mA workload touching a few thousand slots pays for 128 MB of zeroing it never
  reads. This is a fixed per-process cost, invisible in interactions/sec but the
  dominant term for short reductions -- which is most of them.[0m


